  "query_preview": "..\nToday through tonight: Rain is currently moving across the eastern \nthird of Washington and the ID Panhandle. A warm frontal \npassage will result in this rainfall lasting through this \nafternoon and evening. The majority of the forecast area apart \nfrom far SE Washington and Central Idaho has a 60 percent chance\nor higher of seeing 0.10 inches of rain through this evening. \nThe highest amounts are expected across the Cascades, with \ntotals nearing an inch. Portions of the Upper Columbia Basin, \nincluding the Davenport to Coulee City area, have probabilities \nof 50 percent and greater for receiving at least a quarter inch.\nWith some sensitive burn scars in the Cascades, there is a low \nthreat of flooding and potential debris flows. Snow levels will \nremain above 6000 feet for the duration of the event, keeping \nsnowfall confined to the highest elevations and limiting impacts\nto mountain passes. \n\nWednesday through Thursday: Upper-level ridging will build over the \narea midweek, resulting in a warming and drying trend. This, along \nwith the warm frontal passage, will result in much warmer than \nnormal temperatures for this time of year. Expected high \ntemperatures on Wednesday will be in the 50s to 60s, especially from \nthe Spokane area southward. Typical high temperatures in mid-\nNovember are in the 40 to 50s. Low temperatures are roughly 10 \ndegrees above normal, in the 30s to 40s rather than 20s to 30s. \nThere is the potential for some valley fog each morning, \nparticularly on Wednesday morning following the rain today \n(Tuesday). \n\nFriday through Monday: As the ridge begins shifting eastward, a low \npressure system will move through Friday into Saturday, followed by \nanother system Sunday into Monday. Ensemble cluster agreement is \nstrong for both systems, with only minor differences in location and \nstrength. These systems will usher colder air in and allow \ntemperatures to moderate through the weekend, though they will still \noverall be above normal for this time of year, with highs in the 50s \nrather than the low to mid 40s. Snow levels will lower to around \n5000 feet on Saturday, and even further to 4000 feet by Monday \nmorning, resulting in snow at pass levels. Right now, Washington \nPass is currently favored to see the highest snowfall totals, with 2 \nto 8 inches expected. Stevens Pass looks to see up to 2 inches of \nsnow. /AS",
